According to its graph, the one-sided limits at $x=-1$ are different for function $h$ and therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1}h(x)$ doesn't exist.

However, before we jump to any conclusions regarding $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1}\dfrac{f(x)}{h(x)}$, let's consider the one-sided limits:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^-}f(x)=\lim_{x\to -1^+}f(x)=0}
\\\\
&\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^-}h(x)=-1}
\\\\
&\goldD{\lim_{x\to -1^+}h(x)=2}
\end{align}$

We can find each one-sided limit of $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1}\dfrac{f(x)}{h(x)}$ using the fact that the limit of a quotient of functions is the quotient of the individual limits (as long as the limit in the denominator isn't equal to $0$):

$\begin{align}
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^-}\dfrac{f(x)}{h(x)}=\dfrac{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^-}f(x)}{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^-}h(x)}
\\\\
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^+}\dfrac{f(x)}{h(x)}=\dfrac{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^+}f(x)}{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^+}h(x)}
\end{align}$

Let's find the limit from the left:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^-}\dfrac{f(x)}{h(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^-}f(x)}}{\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^-}h(x)}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD 0}{\maroonD {-1}}
\\\\
&=0
\end{align}$

Now let's find the limit from the right:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^+}\dfrac{f(x)}{h(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^+}f(x)}}{\goldD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1^+}h(x)}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD 0}{\goldD 2}
\\\\
&=0
\end{align}$

The one-sided limits are both equal to $0$ and therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -1}\dfrac{f(x)}{h(x)}=0$.
